            <ArticleTitle>In situ nitrogen doping of TiO2 by plasma enhanced atomic layer deposition for enhanced sodium storage performance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13101-13107</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c7dt03303f</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Nitrogen-doped TiO2 is in situ synthesized by plasma enhanced atomic layer deposition on carbon nanotubes (N-TiO2/CNTs). The prepared N-TiO2/CNT nanocomposites are employed as anode materials for sodium ion batteries. The specific capacity of an N-TiO2/CNT electrode is 1.45 times higher than that of a TiO2/CNT electrode at a current density of 50 mA g-1 after 200 cycles. It is demonstrated that N-TiO2/CNTs are more effective in an electrochemical Na cell due to the enhanced kinetics which results from the nitrogen-doping and the amorphous feature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
